{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790168594,"nonce":"9F0+EvUSTYpekxwHk3Ogc/fIfv8=","title":"The native-plugin buffer/enum implementation exposes a remaining Zyntax adapter boundary. Haxe and Wren can use GC-owned Buffer and Enum\u003cT\u003e values, but Zyntax frontends cannot yet consume equivalent foreign values safely.","message":"Evidence in this checkout:\n- crates/caribou-zyntax/src/dispatch.rs: crosses() rejects HOBJ, HARRAY, HFUN and HDYN for parameters and results.\n- crates/caribou-zyntax/src/publish.rs: kind_of() maps aggregate types to these rejected kinds; enum constructor and foreign buffer metadata have no frontend mapping.\n- ../zyntax/crates/zyntax_embed/src/runtime/mod.rs: install_sdk_allocator() sends SDK allocations to zyntax_alloc/zyntax_free.\n- ../zyntax/crates/compiler/src/pool_alloc.rs and collector.rs: Zyntax has its own pool and conservative collector. The collector can register root ranges, but the inspected integration does not trace Caribou objects held inside Zyntax values or retain Zyntax allocations held by Caribou. The old Caribou adapter comment saying there is no collector is stale.\n\nDo not work around this with per-byte conversions or by passing Caribou pointers to Zyntax's release/free paths. The core Buffer descriptor already exposes len/index/set_index/iterate over shared storage. Core enum descriptors carry ordered constructor metadata and traced Value payloads. Reuse these facilities and the existing object/closure lifetime conventions.\n\nRequired follow-up:\n1. Define host-owned foreign-value representation, root/trace/release behavior, and allocator ownership with Zyntax's runtime.\n2. Preserve byte-buffer backing storage and length across calls; return existing foreign objects through their original refs.\n3. Map enum constructor metadata, tags, and payloads through the frontend's native representation and report wrong types without calling incompatible native code.\n4. Cover Rust/plugin -\u003e frontend -\u003e plugin, shared mutation, captured/retained values, and collection in both runtimes, including the Python frontend and ZynML.\n\nThe sibling runtime was inspected; no allocator switch was made because the ownership/trace contract must come first. This is an existing adapter limitation, not a claim that the new Haxe/Wren buffer support already implements native Zyntax conversions.","files":null}]}